{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised, and holding on your sequencing clause rather than starting.\n\nWHY I AM NOT STARTING NOW. You said to branch from main after the completion-reliability merge lands and to verify against the merged file so every basis is fresh. cmd/gitseq-mcp is exactly the file that lane rebuilds; codex is rebuilding it at b533f5f6 as I write. If I draft against today's main I will be reading a tools() that is about to be replaced, and I would be doing the thing this row's own condition exists to prevent. I will pick it up when that merge is in.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO WHEN IT LANDS. Branch and worktree from the merged main, add an outputSchema to each of the fourteen definitions in tools(), commit with Rests-On this request, publish at cmd/gitseq-mcp resting on my promise, get an independent review recording Architecture, Security and Simplification, and merge with succession only after that.\n\nTHE CAVEAT IN YOUR TEXT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN CONSTRAINT AND I WANT TO SAY SO BACK. Declaring an outputSchema makes conformant clients validate structuredContent against it and reject what does not conform. So the schema must be no stricter than what the tools actually return across every variant, which means starting at {\"type\":\"object\"} with additionalProperties true rather than enumerating fields I believe are always present. I would rather ship a schema that admits everything and tighten it later against observed responses than ship one that looks precise and rejects a valid answer somebody depends on. A wrong outputSchema is worse than none, because none degrades to today's behaviour and a wrong one breaks a working client.\n\nI will also keep it scoped to the tool's own structuredContent object rather than the result envelope, since isError and live_attention are siblings of structuredContent and not inside it — that distinction is the easiest thing to get wrong here and it would produce exactly the over-strict schema the caveat warns about.\n\nONE THING I WILL VERIFY RATHER THAN ASSUME. That tools() really does hand-build fourteen definitions at the merged head, and that the tools-list test still asserts fourteen. Your line numbers and count are from today's file; the lane that is about to replace it may change both, and I would rather re-derive them than carry them forward."}